// Sequential, self-contained career courses. Each module teaches core concepts before asking the student to practice.
export const careerTeaching={
am:[
['Course 1 · Investment objectives and mandates','An investment mandate is the assignment a portfolio is expected to fulfill: the goal, eligible investments, benchmark, time horizon, liquidity needs, risk limits and any legal or client constraints. “Grow capital” is too vague to evaluate. A mandate makes the success criteria explicit and prevents a manager from changing the goal after seeing the result.','Before choosing a security, translate the mandate into measurable constraints. A retirement-income fund, an endowment and a short-term cash portfolio have different jobs. A benchmark is a comparison matched to the mandate, not a target that guarantees future performance.','Write a six-line mandate for a fictional college scholarship fund. Include horizon, cash needs, benchmark and one constraint.'],
['Course 2 · Security research and investment theses','A research thesis links evidence about a business to a view about future cash flows and the price investors pay for them. Start with the business model: who pays, for what, how often, and what drives cost. Read filings, earnings reports and industry information. Separate reported facts from management guidance, analyst assumptions and your own estimates.','A strong thesis includes a base case, a plausible downside, valuation sensitivity and evidence that could prove it wrong. Revenue growth alone is not enough: growth that requires heavy spending or destroys margins may not create value. The stock price also reflects expectations, so an excellent company may still be a poor investment at an unjustified price.','Build a one-page thesis for a familiar public company. Label every statement fact, estimate or opinion; identify two falsifying observations.'],
['Course 3 · Risk, valuation and portfolio construction','Risk has several meanings: permanent loss, volatility, drawdown, liquidity, concentration, credit and uncertainty about estimates. A manager selects measures relevant to the mandate rather than treating a single statistic as “risk.” Valuation compares price with estimated economic value; a discounted cash flow model, comparable companies and asset-based analysis each depend on different assumptions.','Position sizing asks how much a holding could affect the whole portfolio if the thesis is wrong. Correlations, sector exposures and shared economic drivers matter: ten holdings can still form one concentrated bet. Diversification can reduce specific risks but does not eliminate broad market risk or ensure a gain.','Show how a 5% position falling 50% changes a hypothetical portfolio before other effects. Name a correlated exposure it might hide.'],
['Course 4 · Benchmarks, performance and attribution','Compare return with an appropriate benchmark over the same dates and with consistent treatment of dividends, fees and currency. Time-weighted returns help assess investment performance while reducing the effect of client deposits and withdrawals; money-weighted returns reflect the investor’s cash-flow timing. Neither number alone explains the result.','Performance attribution breaks results into sources such as asset allocation, security selection and interaction. A single strong period may reflect luck, a market tailwind or concentrated exposure rather than repeatable skill. Evaluate multiple periods and market environments while considering risk, cost, turnover and whether the process stayed within mandate.','Choose a fictional active fund and benchmark. List the data needed before claiming the manager added skill.'],
['Course 5 · Portfolio operations, ethics and a first-year role','Daily work includes reading research, updating models, reviewing exposure and liquidity, preparing investment-committee materials, documenting decisions and monitoring limits. Operations keep cash, trades, prices and corporate actions accurate. A brilliant thesis cannot fix a reconciliation error or an unexamined conflict of interest.','Follow the employer’s compliance policies for personal trading, material nonpublic information, gifts, communications and recordkeeping. Escalate a mistake promptly. For entry roles, show disciplined research, careful spreadsheet checks, curiosity, concise writing and the ability to explain what would change your conclusion.','Prepare a two-minute committee memo: objective, evidence, valuation range, main risk, position-size logic and trigger to revisit.']
],
ib:[
['Course 1 · Financial statements and company analysis','Banking analysis begins with the business and its statements. The income statement reports revenue and expenses over a period; the balance sheet reports assets, liabilities and equity at a point in time; the cash-flow statement reconciles cash generated and used. Net income is not cash. Depreciation, working capital and capital spending help explain the gap.','Build a simple operating story: units sold × average price produces revenue; costs and investment convert revenue into earnings and cash. Read footnotes for debt, leases, contingencies and segment details. In an interview, explain the connection between statements rather than memorizing isolated terms.','Trace a $10 increase in depreciation through all three statements, stating your tax assumption.'],
['Course 2 · Valuation foundations','Enterprise value represents value attributable to all capital providers; equity value is the value attributable to shareholders. A simplified bridge is enterprise value minus debt and other claims plus cash, subject to details. Multiples such as EV/EBITDA compare value with an operating measure, but only make sense when firms, periods, accounting and growth prospects are sufficiently comparable.','A discounted cash flow estimates the present value of future free cash flows using assumptions for growth, margins, capital spending, working capital and a discount rate. Small changes can produce large changes in value, especially in the terminal period. Valuation is a range supported by scenarios, not a precise fact.','For a fictional retailer, name three drivers for a DCF and explain why one is uncertain.'],
['Course 3 · Transactions, M&A and deal logic','An acquisition can create value through cost savings, additional sales, capabilities or strategic positioning, but the buyer may overpay or underestimate execution risk. Accretion/dilution analysis estimates the effect on earnings per share under a defined financing and synergy case. A deal that is accretive in the first year is not automatically attractive or value-creating.','A merger model combines operating forecasts, purchase price, financing, fees, purchase accounting and potential synergies. Sensitize assumptions such as premium, debt cost, timing and integration expense. Distinguish management’s announced synergy target from achieved and independently supportable savings.','Create a base and downside scenario for a hypothetical acquisition. Identify which assumption most affects the conclusion.'],
['Course 4 · Capital raising and the deal process','Companies raise equity to share ownership and risk, or debt to borrow with repayment obligations. A financing decision depends on use of proceeds, cost, flexibility, dilution, credit capacity and market conditions. Advisors coordinate analysis and process; lawyers, accountants, regulators and the issuer each have distinct responsibilities.','A transaction process includes preparation, diligence, valuation, marketing or negotiation, documentation, approvals and closing. Timelines vary. Information barriers and confidentiality protect clients and markets. Never circulate material nonpublic information outside approved channels; escalate a suspected disclosure immediately.','Explain how choosing debt instead of equity changes both risk and ownership for a fictional company.'],
['Course 5 · Analyst craft, ethics and recruiting','Analysts build and check models, prepare client materials, coordinate comments and maintain accurate versions. Use clear labels, units, sources and formulas; independently check key outputs. Under deadline pressure, communicate an error as soon as it is found rather than concealing or silently patching it.','Interviewers test accounting, valuation, deal judgment, teamwork and resilience. Structure responses: define the problem, state assumptions, perform the analysis, explain the result and acknowledge limitations. Employer examples and recruiting dates vary; check official postings and ask informed questions about team work and training.','Prepare a three-minute company overview and a separate answer for why this role fits your evidence-based interests.']
],
wm:[
['Course 1 · Discovery, goals and the client relationship','Wealth management begins with listening. A discovery conversation identifies goals, family circumstances, cash flows, debts, assets, upcoming needs, legal structures and concerns. Ask open questions and confirm meaning before making a plan. “I want to be secure” needs definition: what event, by when, and what tradeoffs feel acceptable?','Document information with permission, protect confidentiality and distinguish verified facts from estimates. A client’s financial capacity to take risk can differ from emotional willingness to tolerate it. A planner should understand both rather than label a client from one questionnaire score.','Draft six respectful discovery questions for a fictional client nearing graduation.'],
['Course 2 · Portfolio foundations and risk','A portfolio is designed around time horizon, liquidity, risk capacity, risk tolerance and objectives. Stocks, bonds and cash-like holdings have different expected behavior and risks. Diversification spreads exposures, while asset allocation determines the broad mix. Neither guarantees a positive return; clients can lose money, including near-term money invested in volatile assets.','Compare fees, taxes, turnover, concentration and underlying holdings. A diversified fund may still hold a narrow segment; different funds may overlap. Rebalancing can restore target exposures but may create taxes or transaction costs in taxable accounts. Explain assumptions and tradeoffs instead of presenting a forecast as certainty.','A client needs tuition money next year and retirement money in decades. Explain why these goals deserve separate analysis.'],
['Course 3 · Planning across cash flow, debt and protection','A financial plan coordinates spending, emergency reserves, debt, insurance, taxes, education goals and retirement. Cash-flow analysis reveals timing: a household can have positive annual income but still face a short-term cash gap. Debt review includes interest rates, repayment terms, collateral and consequences of missed payments.','Insurance transfers specified risks under contract terms; it does not protect against every loss. Risk-management conversations consider the client’s exposure, existing coverage, exclusions and affordability. Complex legal or tax matters may require qualified specialists; coordinating referrals is part of responsible work.','Create a simple priority map for a fictional student with income, credit-card debt and a near-term move. State what information is missing.'],
['Course 4 · Retirement, taxes and estate coordination','Retirement planning estimates income needs, savings, timing, employer benefits and possible withdrawal strategies under uncertain returns and longevity. Account labels have distinct tax and distribution rules. Roth and traditional IRA treatment depends on eligibility, contribution rules and individual circumstances; the same choice does not fit everyone.','Tax planning considers when income, deductions, gains and distributions occur. Estate planning coordinates beneficiaries, ownership and documents with qualified legal professionals. A beneficiary designation can matter independently of a will. Explain general concepts, verify current rules and avoid giving tax or legal conclusions outside your role.','Explain why a retirement projection should include scenarios instead of one “expected” balance.'],
['Course 5 · Advice, ethics and professional practice','Recommendations should connect documented goals and constraints to a reasoned strategy. Explain material costs, risks, alternatives and conflicts in language the client can understand. Suitability, fiduciary and other duties depend on the capacity and applicable rules; learn the exact obligations for the role and firm.','An effective advisor follows up, tracks implementation and revisits the plan after life changes. Keep records, protect data and escalate concerns. Entry paths may involve licensing, supervised experience or credentials depending on services. Verify requirements with regulators and credentialing organizations rather than relying on hearsay.','Role-play explaining a proposed change, including its cost, one downside and a reason the client might decline.']
],
fp:[
['Course 1 · The planning process','Financial planning is a repeatable process: understand circumstances, clarify goals, analyze the current path, develop options, communicate recommendations, support implementation and monitor changes. A financial plan is not just a portfolio. It connects cash flow, risk management, investing, tax, retirement, education and estate considerations.','Turn broad wishes into goals with amounts, dates and priorities, while noting uncertainty. Ask what success means to the client. Identify missing facts and assumptions before modeling. A projection can reveal tradeoffs, but it cannot predict market returns or guarantee an outcome.','Write a fictional client’s three goals as measurable statements and list information needed for each.'],
['Course 2 · Cash flow, saving and debt','Cash-flow planning compares income timing with fixed, variable and irregular expenses. Liquidity helps cover near-term needs; an emergency reserve is not the same as a long-term investment portfolio. Savings targets depend on job stability, responsibilities, insurance and access to support. Avoid universal “one-size” rules without context.','Debt analysis compares interest, repayment schedules, tax treatment where relevant, collateral and behavioral constraints. Paying off high-cost debt can reduce a known financing cost, but a plan also considers liquidity and employer benefits. Make assumptions visible and discuss alternatives without shaming a client.','Build a monthly cash-flow map with irregular costs converted to monthly reserves.'],
['Course 3 · Investing and risk for planners','Investment planning starts with objectives, horizon, liquidity needs, risk capacity and tolerance. Asset allocation describes broad exposures; diversification reduces dependence on individual holdings but cannot remove market risk. The plan should explain why a strategy fits the goal and what could make it fail.','Compare investments using costs, tax characteristics, liquidity, diversification and risks. Market returns are uncertain; use ranges and stress scenarios rather than promises. Rebalancing is a discipline for returning to a chosen exposure and may have costs or tax consequences.','Explain to a fictional client why a diversified portfolio can still decline during a broad selloff.'],
['Course 4 · Retirement and account tax basics','Retirement analysis estimates spending, income sources, savings, timing, longevity and possible withdrawal patterns. Employer plans, IRAs and taxable brokerage accounts differ in tax treatment, access and rules. For IRAs, annual contribution limits, compensation and income/deduction eligibility matter; confirm official rules for the applicable year.','Model scenarios for savings rate, retirement date and returns. Show which variables the client controls and which they cannot. A plan can be robust across several cases without being certain. Coordinate tax advice with current rules and a qualified tax professional where the question requires individualized analysis.','Describe a base, lower-return and delayed-retirement scenario; state how each changes the plan.'],
['Course 5 · Insurance, estate and implementation','Risk planning asks which losses would be financially disruptive and what contractual coverage exists. Review deductibles, exclusions, limits and premium affordability. Estate planning coordinates goals, ownership, beneficiaries and legal documents; legal instruments and state rules require qualified legal advice. A planner recognizes where specialist expertise is needed.','Implementation converts recommendations into clear next steps, responsible parties and dates. Monitoring tracks progress, changes in circumstances and whether assumptions still hold. Ethical practice includes confidentiality, competence, transparent compensation and clear disclosure of conflicts under the rules applicable to the professional.','Prepare a one-page implementation checklist that identifies who will act, when and what must be verified.']
],
cf:[
['Course 1 · How a company makes and uses money','Corporate finance asks how an organization generates cash, funds operations and allocates capital. Learn the revenue model, customer economics, cost structure and working-capital cycle. An FP&A analyst connects operational drivers—units, prices, staffing, occupancy—to the financial statements. A spreadsheet is a model of those drivers, not the business itself.','The income statement shows performance over a period; the balance sheet captures resources and obligations at a date; the cash-flow statement explains cash sources and uses. Profit and cash can diverge because of noncash expenses, receivables, inventory, payables and investment.','Map a familiar company’s customer activity to revenue, direct costs, operating costs and cash collection.'],
['Course 2 · Budgeting, forecasting and variance','A budget is an approved plan; a forecast is the current estimate of likely results; a target is an intended outcome. Confusing them hides risk. Build a driver-based forecast from explicit assumptions, link formulas and state the data date. Use scenarios to show a range when the future is uncertain.','Variance analysis compares actual with plan or prior forecast, then searches for drivers: volume, price, mix, timing, rate and execution. Determine whether a variance is temporary or structural before proposing action. A favorable variance can still signal a problem, such as delayed maintenance or underspending on hiring.','Analyze a fictional 12% revenue shortfall. Ask what changed in price, units, mix and timing before revising the forecast.'],
['Course 3 · Unit economics and operating decisions','Unit economics examine revenue and costs per customer, product, transaction or location. Contribution margin is revenue minus variable costs attributable to the unit; it helps estimate how volume affects operating results. Fixed costs, capacity limits, acquisition costs and customer retention can alter the decision.','Break-even analysis estimates the activity level at which contribution covers fixed costs under stated assumptions. A positive contribution margin does not guarantee company profit if overhead, financing or capital investment is large. Always label the scope, period and assumptions behind a metric.','Calculate break-even volume for a fictional product and identify one cost that may not remain fixed.'],
['Course 4 · Capital allocation, working capital and valuation','Management allocates capital among operations, hiring, research, acquisitions, debt reduction and shareholder distributions. Evaluate expected cash generation, uncertainty, strategic fit and alternative uses of funds. Net present value discounts future expected cash flows; its result is sensitive to timing, discount rate and forecast quality.','Working capital links cash to receivables, inventory and payables. Growth can consume cash if customers pay slowly or inventory rises ahead of demand. A company can report profits while facing cash pressure. Use cash conversion and liquidity alongside earnings when assessing a plan.','Explain why a fast-growing business might need more cash even when sales are rising.'],
['Course 5 · Communicating with leaders and controls','Decision support starts with the question, not a spreadsheet. State the recommendation or finding, quantify the impact, show assumptions and alternatives, then identify what would change the answer. A concise executive summary should be traceable to underlying data. Use charts that make units, periods and comparisons explicit.','Finance teams protect data integrity through reconciliations, version control, access controls and review. Do not overwrite source data or conceal forecast changes. When a number is wrong, flag its impact and correct the record. Interviewers value thoughtful business judgment, communication and careful model checks.','Create a one-slide decision memo for a fictional equipment purchase with base and downside cases.']
],
er:[
['Course 1 · Company, industry and filings','Equity research studies a public company’s economics, competitive position and value. Begin with the business model, customer, product, revenue segments and cost drivers. Industry structure—substitutes, suppliers, barriers, regulation and bargaining power—helps explain whether profits may persist. Then read filings and earnings materials to test the narrative.','A 10-K annual filing and 10-Q quarterly filing include statements, risk disclosures and management discussion. Read notes and segment data, not only headlines. Distinguish historical results, management guidance and your forecast. Public information can be incomplete or backward-looking, so document uncertainty.','Select a company and write: who pays, what they buy, why they return and what could disrupt the model.'],
['Course 2 · Forecasting revenue, margins and cash','A forecast decomposes revenue into operational drivers. For a subscription company, customers × average revenue per customer may be a start; churn, new additions and pricing affect the result. For a retailer, stores, comparable sales, traffic, units and average price may matter. Choose drivers that explain the business rather than applying a generic growth rate.','Forecast costs and margins in relation to their drivers. Consider fixed versus variable costs, capacity, input prices, competition and reinvestment. Reconcile earnings to operating cash flow and capital expenditure. State whether estimates are nominal, the currency and period, and what data supports each major assumption.','Build a simple three-year revenue bridge with units, price and retention. Identify the weakest assumption.'],
['Course 3 · Valuation and market expectations','Valuation estimates what future cash flows or earnings might be worth today under assumptions. Comparable multiples summarize how markets price a peer group, but peer selection, accounting, growth, margins and capital structure matter. A discounted cash-flow model makes cash-flow assumptions explicit and discounts them for time and risk.','The market price embeds expectations. Ask what must happen for today’s price to make sense rather than simply labeling a stock cheap or expensive by one ratio. Sensitivity tables and scenarios show uncertainty. A price target is an analytical output, not a guaranteed result or personal recommendation.','Compare two firms on EV/EBITDA and explain at least three reasons the multiple difference could be justified.'],
['Course 4 · Thesis, catalysts and risk','A research thesis combines business facts, forecast, valuation, expected drivers and risks. A catalyst is an event that may reveal information or change expectations; it is not necessarily positive. Risks include competitive, operational, regulatory, financial, governance and valuation risks. Probability and impact matter; listing risks alone is not analysis.','Track evidence that would confirm or disconfirm each claim. Update estimates when new facts arrive and explain what changed. Avoid anchoring on an old target, cherry-picking comparable firms or confusing a persuasive story with evidence. Good research is falsifiable and clearly separates facts from judgment.','Write a bull, base and bear case with one measurable trigger for revisiting each.'],
['Course 5 · Research communication and professional standards','A research note communicates the conclusion, key evidence, estimates, valuation, risk and uncertainty to a specific audience. Lead with what is decision-relevant, then show the reasoning. Charts need labeled axes, time periods, units and sources. Be candid about limitations and conflicts.','Research roles may have legal and firm-specific obligations. Use only permitted information; respect information barriers and confidentiality, and never trade or tip based on material nonpublic information. Junior analysts contribute careful data gathering, model maintenance, earnings notes and clear questions. Verify role requirements with each employer.','Deliver a five-minute earnings update: surprise versus expectations, driver, forecast revision, valuation implication and remaining uncertainty.']
],
risk:[
['Course 1 · Risk taxonomy and the risk cycle','Risk management identifies uncertainty that could prevent an organization from meeting its goals. Market risk comes from changes in prices or rates; credit risk from a counterparty’s failure to pay; liquidity risk from difficulty meeting cash needs or selling an asset; operational risk from people, processes, systems or external events. Model and compliance risk also matter.','The cycle is identify, assess, measure, control, monitor and communicate. A risk register assigns ownership, likelihood, impact and mitigation, but scoring is a prioritization aid—not an objective prediction. Look for interconnected exposures: a system outage can become an operational, liquidity and reputational event.','Classify a payment-system outage and trace two secondary risks it could trigger.'],
['Course 2 · Measurement, distributions and stress','A metric must match the question. Volatility measures historical dispersion, not maximum loss. Value at Risk estimates a loss threshold over a horizon and confidence level under a model; it does not describe how severe losses may be beyond that threshold. Expected shortfall focuses on average losses in the tail under specified assumptions.','Stress tests examine severe scenarios, including shocks that have not occurred in the sample. Results depend on portfolio positions, correlations, liquidity and scenario design. Historical data can miss structural change. Backtesting and independent model validation compare model behavior with outcomes and known limitations.','Explain why a 99% one-day VaR does not mean the remaining 1% loss is capped at the VaR amount.'],
['Course 3 · Credit, market and liquidity cases','Credit analysis studies willingness and ability to repay: cash flow, leverage, collateral, covenants, industry conditions and downside recovery. Market risk examines exposure to rates, equities, FX, commodities and volatility. Liquidity has funding and market dimensions; an asset may be sellable in normal times yet hard to sell during stress.','Use sensitivity analysis to identify which variables drive exposure. Consider basis risk: a hedge and the underlying exposure may not move together. Net exposure can conceal gross concentration, counterparty dependency or collateral calls. Limits need clear escalation and monitoring rules.','Analyze a fictional borrower under a revenue decline and higher rates. Name the early-warning indicators you would track.'],
['Course 4 · Controls, governance and incident response','A control reduces the likelihood or impact of a risk: authorization, separation of duties, reconciliation, access restriction, alerts, recovery testing and backup procedures. Controls can fail or create false confidence. Test whether the control operates, whether evidence exists and whether exceptions are followed up.','Governance assigns risk ownership, oversight and escalation. Independent challenge can surface optimistic assumptions. During an incident, preserve evidence, notify appropriate teams, limit harm and communicate verified facts. Do not make unsupported claims or delay escalation because a metric looks normal.','Sketch a control chain for accidental duplicate payments, including detection and remediation.'],
['Course 5 · Models, communication and the analyst role','Model risk includes errors in data, design, implementation, use and interpretation. Validate inputs, formulas, performance, stability and use boundaries; compare with a simple benchmark. A model can be technically correct yet unsuitable for a changed market or a different decision. Document version, owner, assumptions and limitations.','A risk memo gives exposure, context, plausible impact, evidence, uncertainty, proposed action and escalation owner. Be precise about what is known and what is inferred. Early-career analysts need quantitative fluency, skepticism, careful documentation and the confidence to raise a concern constructively.','Write an incident update separating observed fact, possible consequence, immediate control and open question.']
],
quant:[
['Course 1 · Probability, statistics and market data','Quantitative finance uses mathematics and computation to study prices, risk and decisions. Begin with probability distributions, expected value, variance, covariance and conditional probability. A sample mean estimates a population quantity but can be noisy; financial returns often have extreme observations and changing distributions.','Understand the data before fitting a model: timestamps, corporate actions, survivorship, missing observations and units. Returns are not the same as price levels. Log and simple returns differ; compounding across periods is multiplicative. Align calendars and avoid using information that was unavailable at the decision time.','Given three sequential returns, explain why cumulative return is not their simple arithmetic sum.'],
['Course 2 · Portfolio mathematics and factor exposure','Portfolio expected return is a weighted combination of asset expectations; portfolio variance depends on each asset’s variance and cross-asset covariance. Low correlation can reduce volatility, but correlations change. The efficient-frontier idea compares possible risk-return combinations under assumptions; inputs are estimates, not known facts.','Factor models describe common drivers such as market, size, value or rates. Exposure analysis helps distinguish a manager’s claimed skill from broad systematic exposure. Leverage, turnover, transaction costs and constraints change the practical result. Validate whether a mathematical solution is investable.','Compare two portfolios with identical holdings weights except correlation assumptions; explain why the risk estimate changes.'],
['Course 3 · Time series, regression and uncertainty','Regression estimates relationships in observed data. Correlation does not prove causation, and coefficients can shift across periods. Examine residuals, confidence intervals, multicollinearity, outliers and stability. Time-series data require attention to serial correlation, nonstationarity and look-ahead bias.','A backtest must follow the information timeline, use realistic prices and account for costs, liquidity, corporate actions and constraints. Split training, validation and test periods chronologically. Repeatedly tuning parameters against a test set leaks information and makes apparent performance unreliable.','Design a chronological train-validation-test split and name one leakage risk for each stage.'],
['Course 4 · Derivatives, pricing and hedging intuition','A derivative’s value depends on an underlying variable and contract terms. Options provide asymmetric payoffs: a call gives the holder the right, not obligation, to buy at a strike; a put gives the right to sell. Premium, strike, expiry, volatility, rates and dividends influence value. An option can expire worthless.','A hedge reduces a specified exposure but can introduce basis, counterparty, liquidity, model and rollover risk. Greeks approximate sensitivities—delta to underlying price, gamma to delta changes, vega to volatility and theta to time—under a model. Sensitivities are local approximations, not guarantees for large moves.','Draw a call payoff at expiration and distinguish payoff from profit after premium.'],
['Course 5 · Research engineering, validation and communication','Quant roles combine mathematics, software engineering and domain judgment. Tests, code review, reproducibility, version control and data lineage help prevent silent errors. Benchmark a method against a simple baseline and measure performance after realistic costs. Monitor production behavior and define when to disable a strategy.','Present the model’s purpose, data, assumptions, validation, failure modes and decision boundaries. Avoid overstating precision. In interviews, explain your reasoning, verify edge cases and state what additional evidence you need. Employers differ: some emphasize research, others implementation, execution, risk or systems.','Propose a validation checklist for a forecasting model and include a drift monitor and rollback condition.']
]};
